  "subtitle": "[ASSET: left | SheFainted | width=51%] This month's VMA goes to [LINK:1963:JIM_99_thor0|JIM #99|2027-02-06], where we find Jane Foster, having just witnessed Don Blake getting thrown out of an upper-story window...\n[BR]\n[BR] The caption reads:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=And then, because she <i>is</i> a female...]\n[BR]\n[BR] Mr. Hyde says, \"She <b><i>fainted</i></b>!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=(OMG!)]",
  "review_text": "Not a stellar month for Marvel, with eight graded stories spread out over six books (both <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> and <i>Strange Tales</i> had two), averaging only a middling [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=4.51], only a hair higher than  last month's <b>4.35.</b>\n\nThe two full-sized magazines came through (at least on their main stories, though Spider-Man stumbled in the second half).&nbsp; These were the only two that exceeded mid-grade this time around (though neither eclipsed it by much).&nbsp; What followed were readable enough tales featuring Iron Man, Thor, and even Giant-Man/Wasp this time around.\n\nBut the bottom rung was brutal, with both parts of <i>Strange Tales</i> 116, in particular, dragging everything down (as far as I'm concerned, Doctor Strange is still entirely a no-show), and the Spider-Man/Human Torch battle should probably never have been done in the first place.\n\nLet's face it -- Marvel's quality this month was inconsistent, providing no true gems... and a couple of major duds.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nOn a completely different topic... having both the Wasp and the Watcher serve as storytellers in the backup features for this month's <i>Tales to Astonish</i> and <i>Tales of Suspense</i> issues (respectively) was... <i>well</i>... [SPAN: severity=info; text=interesting.]\n\nIt strikes me that the Watcher serves as the more compelling narrator for the simple reason that his insight is <i>presumably</i> more trustworthy.&nbsp; Not only that, it leaves open the question [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Is he describing an actual event in our universe?\"] (as opposed to Jan, who is obviously making up her own tale that she claims \"takes place in the year two thousand\" -- still <i>well</i> in the future from her perspective).\n\nThe role of narrator is actually a good use for the supposedly-dispassionate Watcher, who obviously knows a lot of things because of all the \"watching\" he's done over the centuries, and it's established early on that he'll be put to use in that capacity for a lot of these kinds of stories.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: Not to mention his eventual and excellent use as the narrator of <i>What If?</i> In the 1970s.]\n\nAs a matter of fact (and I realize this could be classified as violating [PAGE: scenario], but, since this is a monthly summary that is allowed to zoom out on history a bit...), the Announcements section of next month's <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> #9 (ASM #9) indicates the publishers are \"getting real fond of the Watcher\" and hope they \"can find a way to feature him a bit more than we're doing at present.\"\n\nFrom a marketing perspective, both narrator roles provide little more than a bit of extra window dressing around \"tales\" readers may have gotten <i>anyway</i> (either to \"astonish,\" as in the case of Jan, or keep us in \"suspense,\" as in the case of the Watcher).&nbsp; But in the end, it seems they're intended to steer readers <i>away</i> from these backup tales and <i>toward</i> the characters who make up the Marvel Universe at large.\n\nThe whole thing seems intended to gradually drive out these backup stories in favor of new things...\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Stay tuned...]\n\n[DEVNOTE: *** SUMMARY_BEGIN ***\n*** THIS MONTH ***8 graded stories over 6 issues...TOTAL WEIGHT: 4.58 grade character issue   weight 6.5   asm       ASM #8  0.75 6.5   ff        FF #22  1.00 4.5   iron      TOS #49 0.75 4.0   thor      JIM #99 0.50 4.0   giant     TTA #51 0.50 2.0   asm       ASM #8  0.25 2.0   strange   ST #116 0.33 1.5   torch     ST #116 0.504.51*** LAST MONTH ***9 graded stories over 8 issues...TOTAL WEIGHT: 6.58 grade character issue   weight 7.0   asm       ASM #7  1.00 6.0   thor      JIM #98 0.50 6.0   giant     TTA #50 0.50 5.5   strange   ST #115 0.33 5.0   torch     ST #115 0.50 4.0   ff        FF #21  1.00 4.0   iron      TOS #48 0.75 2.5   xmen      XMEN #2 1.00 1.8   aven      AVEN #2 1.004.35\n*** SUMMARY_END ***]",
  "letters_page": "[HANG0] * Introduction of Mr. Hyde\n[HANG0] * Peter Parker punching Flash Thompson in the boxing ring\n[HANG0] * Sue Storm finally getting some offensive powers\n\n[DEVNOTE: *** INTRODUCTION_BEGIN ***\nMister Hyde | villain | Milestone: (first), (origin)\nNotes | first | villain | Living Brain\nNotes | first | Book of Vishanti\nNotes | first | Sneepers (race)\n*** INTRODUCTION_END ***]\n\n<b>Roundup:</b>\n[DEVNOTE: *** HIGHLIGHTS_BEGIN ***]\n[HANG0] * Thor and Mr. Hyde square off in a tale where the Asgard drama soars, Dr. Blake plummets, and the villain's entire motivations remain questionable.\n[HANG0] * Giant‑Man, the Wasp, the Human Top, and one very unlucky Red Agent collide in a Cold War whirl.\n[HANG0] * Peter's boxing match with Flash Thompson highlights a story that is otherwise detoured by a runaway robot in the school hallways.\n[HANG0] * Spider‑Man and the Human Torch clash again in a story that <i>completely</i> forgets why we like Peter in the first place.\n[HANG0] * Sue steps up with a meaningful new ability just as the FF are pulled into the Mole Man's latest overcooked scheme.\n[HANG0] * The Human Torch and Thing clash again, dragged down by contrivances, contradictions, and a villain who just won't stay dead.\n[HANG0] * Doctor Strange stumbles through Nightmare's realm, where every mystic \"rule\" feels like it was invented five seconds before he arrived.\n[HANG0] * A personality‑warped Angel tangles with Iron Man, while the X‑Men appear just long enough to wonder why Stark's explosions only affect flying teenagers -- and no buildings.\n[DEVNOTE: *** HIGHLIGHTS_END ***]",
  "ownership": "[HANG0] * Sub-Mariner wanders into the Avengers sphere of influence\n[HANG0] * Thor finishes things up with Mr. Hyde\n[HANG0] * A \"Black Knight\" shows up to menace Giant-Man and the Wasp\n[HANG0] * The X-Men face a huge blob of an enemy\n[HANG0] * Spider-Man is \"shocked\" to meet a new foe\n[HANG0] * The Human Torch gets a second crack at a slippery foe\n[HANG0] * Dr. Doom is back once again\n[HANG0] * and Iron Man faces some dude wearing a <i>bunch</i> of rings!"
